Frames 17–18), or working on exterior panels (Frames 19–22).\n- **No worker is seen assembling, bolting, or stitching seat components.**\n\n---\n\n### 4. **No Connection or Securing Method for Seats**\n- Since no seat assembly is shown, there is no visible method of how seat sections are connected or secured.\n- The only fastening actions visible involve **engine mounts, electrical connectors, or body panels**, not seat frames or upholstery.\n\n---\n\n### What *Is* Shown in the Frames:\n- **Frames 1–3**: Workers installing wiring harnesses in the car’s interior structure.\n- **Frames 4–6**: Assembly of chassis or body components on jigs.\n- **Frames 7–16**: Engine installation and alignment using overhead cranes and jigs.\n- **Frames 17–18**: Interior finishing — cleaning and adjusting trim near the driver’s seat (not assembling the seat).\n- **Frames 19–22**: Final inspection, washing, and presentation of the completed car.\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe provided frames focus on **engine installation, electrical systems, chassis assembly, and final interior/exterior detailing**. The **assembly of car seats — including joining front/back sections, using specific tools, or securing methods — is not shown** in any of the 22 frames. The documentary appears to skip the seat manufacturing or assembly process entirely, likely because it occurs off-camera or in a different facility not featured in these clips.\n'], 'condensed_response': '**Condensed Scene Summary (with character tracking & timestamps)**  \n\nThe 22‑frame excerpt captures a busy automotive assembly line, but **no car‑seat construction is visible**. The camera follows a few recurring crew members as they move from one work‑station to another, handling wiring, chassis components, and engine modules while the interior trim is merely adjusted near the already‑installed seats.\n\n| Frame (≈\u202ftimestamp) | Visible Characters* | Primary Action | Note on Seats |\n|---------------------|----------------------|---